  "answer": "I'll start by exploring the workspace to understand the project.\nFound the bug: `add` subtracts instead of adding. Let me check the runtime available for the existing test.\nBaseline confirmed failing. Now the minimal fix:\nDone. The test now passes.\n\n## What was wrong\n\n`src/add.ts` implemented addition as subtraction:\n\n```ts\nexport function add(a: number, b: number) { return a - b }\n```\n\nThe test's first case already exposed it — `add(2, 3)` returned `-1` instead of `5`. This also broke negative inputs in a subtler way: `add(-2, 3)` would have returned `-5` (expected `1`), and `add(-5, -7)` would have returned `2` (expected `-12`).\n\n## The fix\n\nA one-character correction in the function body:\n\n```ts\nexport function add(a: number, b: number) { return a + b }\n```\n\n## Scope and constraints\n\n- **Public signature preserved** — parameters `(a: number, b: number)` and the inferred `number` return type are untouched; only the operator changed.\n- **Tests untouched** — `test/oracle.test.ts` was not modified.\n- **No config/runner changes** — no files added, no `package.json` edits, no scripts introduced; the existing `bun:test` runner was used as-is via `bun test`.\n- `AGENTS.md`, `package.json`, and `test/oracle.test.ts` are all unchanged; `src/add.ts` is the only file modified.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Step | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `bun test` (before fix) | 0 pass, 1 fail — `Expected: 5, Received: -1` |\n| `bun test` (after fix) | **1 pass, 0 fail**, 5 `expect()` calls |\n| `tsc --noEmit --strict src/add.ts` | clean, exit 0 |\n\nThe test covers five input pairs (`2+3`, `-2+3`, `0+0`, `-5+-7`, `99+1`), so the fix is confirmed against positive, negative, zero, and carry-across-100 cases alike."
